    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Gaucher Disease (GD) is a severe genetic disorder caused by mutations in the gene GBA1 encoding the lysosomal hydrolase glucocerebrosidase (GCase). GCase deficiency leads to glycolipid storage in the lysosomes of macrophages, triggering their transformation in pathological “Gaucher Cells” that infiltrate tissues and promote immune deregulation, ultimately initiating progressive disease in the spleen, liver, bone and brain. Hematopoietic stem cell (HSC) transplantation (HSCT) is known to cure GD through replacing patient blood cells – including macrophages – with donor blood cells, yet comorbidities arising from histoincompatibility between patient and donor prevent its widespread application. Owing to recent advancements in gene-editing technologies, a promising new alternative is ex vivo gene-correction and transplantation of patient-derived HSCs. To this end, we present stable and efficient targeting of GBA1 expression cassettes to a “safe harbor” locus in primary human HSCs using a CRISPR/Cas9 and AAV6 platform. In vitro differentiation of GBA1-targeted HSCs produces functional macrophages with elevated GCase expression, demonstrating the potential for GBA1 gene-targeting to restore GCase activity in GD macrophages. Moreover, we show that it is possible to constrain GBA1 transgene expression to macrophage-lineage cells using a tissue-specific promoter, thereby minimizing ectopic transgene expression in HSCs. Importantly, GBA1-targeted HSCs sustain human hematopoiesis at least 32 weeks in a murine xenograft model and produce circulating monocytes capable of differentiation into macrophages, indicating that they retain proper self-renewal and multi-lineage reconstitution capabilities in vivo. Taken together, these studies provide proof-of-principle for genome-engineered autologous HSCT as a novel therapy for Gaucher Disease and set the stage for the clinical translation of ex vivo genome-editing therapies.",
